{
  "paraId": "4ED8DD70",
  "style": "",
  "text": "The multivariate analysis showed that ypN+ was an independent risk factor for prognosis after NCIT. In subgroup analysis, OS and DFS did not differ significantly between ypN0 and ypN2 + 3 patients regardless of adjuvant immunotherapy. However, ypN1 patients benefited significantly in DFS (P = 0.02) and showed a trend toward OS benefit (P = 0.06), possibly owing to insufficient sample size or short follow-up. ypN0 patients, sensitive to NCIT, may gain little from adjuvant immunotherapy; ypN2 + 3 patients, insensitive to NCIT with a larger preoperative nodal burden, cannot have their prognosis significantly improved by immunotherapy alone, whereas ypN1 may benefit most. OS and DFS did not differ significantly among TRG1, TRG2 and TRG3 patients, but TRG4 patients had an OS benefit from adjuvant immunotherapy (P = 0.002). This is in line with the 8th edition AJCC-ypTNM staging of esophageal cancer[20]. After neoadjuvant therapy, ypN status has a greater impact on prognosis than ypT[19, 29, 30]."
